John Edward: Linguistic skills are necessary for global citizens
Scotland's independent schools have maintained a track record of academic excellence, and it continues to result in another outstanding test in 2018, which is only strengthened by personal and collective success in the efforts of sports, art, music, and other communities.
With more than 30,000 students across Scotland, these schools, represented by the Scottish Council of Independent Schools (SCIS), strive to provide the best level of service to their students and parents.
Independent schools aim to prepare their students for more and higher education, their chosen professions, and their place as global citizens.
As an educational area in which a special school curriculum can also design and apply, we're looking to continue the modern language to be popular and preferred in schools.
Nelson Mandela said: “If you talk to a man in a language that he understands, it’s in his mind.”
"If you talk to him in his own language, it goes to his heart."
It's a powerful reminder that we can't just rely on English if we want to build relationships and trust with people from other countries.
From this year's recent test results, we can see that the languages are at the top in league tables with the highest pass rates in independent schools.
Sixty-eight per cent among foreign language students have achieved higher grade.
Data collected from seventy-seven members of SCIS found that seventy-two percent of students achieved higher grade in Mandarin, while 72 per cent German studies, 69 per cent French studies and 63 per cent studying in Spanish.
This proves that independent schools in Scotland support foreign languages as vital proficities that will undoubtedly require children and young people in the future.
The language is now being held in a similar way as the curriculum of independent schools and anywhere else STEM (Science, Technology, Engineering and Mathematics).
A study by the UK Commission for Employment and Skills 2014 found that 17 per cent blamed their lack of language skills.
So further, language skills are becoming essential for young people to prepare for their future career.
For more potential job opportunities that language is needed, these skills are necessary in the globalized world.
Whoever chooses a career, if they have learned a second language, it will be their real advantage with acquiring their life-threatening skills in the future.
Being able to communicate directly with people abroad automatically puts a multi-linguing person before the competition.
According to YoGov, more than 4,000 adults in the UK in 2013, 75 per cent were unable to speak a foreign language well during the conversation, and the French-decade-number was the only language, 15 per cent.
That's why it's so important for today's children to invest in language education.
Having multiple languages, especially the developmental economy language, will give children a better opportunity to look for meaningful employment.
In Scotland, each school will differ in the languages they taught.
A number of schools will focus on more classic modern languages, on the other hand when it comes to 2020, it can be seen that others will teach languages that are considered the most important for the UK, such as Mandarin or Japanese.
No matter what your child's interests, there will always be many languages to choose in independent schools, with expert teaching staff in this case.
Scottish independent schools are dedicated to providing an educational environment that will prepare the children and create them with the skills they need to succeed, no matter what happens in the future.
In the global business environment, it cannot be denied at the moment that languages play an important role for the future of the country, so it must be reflected in the field of education.
In fact, modern languages should truly be regarded as “international communication skills.””
Independent schools will provide this choice, diversity and encouragement for young people in Scotland.
It has to be done.
John Edward is the Director of the Scottish Council of Independent School
